Body:
Could not reproduce on macOS arm64 with opencode **1.18.4** (same version as reported).

**Harness:** local spec-compliant Streamable HTTP server built on `@modelcontextprotocol/sdk` 1.29.0 `StreamableHTTPServerTransport` (with `sessionIdGenerator`), configured to reject any post-`initialize` request lacking `Mcp-Session-Id` with the exact 400 body from this issue (`Bad Request: No valid session ID provided.`). Config used:

```jsonc
"harness": {
  "type": "remote",
  "url": "http://127.0.0.1:3987/mcp",
  "enabled": true,
  "headers": { "Authorization": "Bearer test-token-123" },
  "timeout": 120000
}
```

**Server-side log for a single `opencode run` invocation that called the tool:**

```text
[srv] POST method=initialize mcp-session-id=<MISSING>
[srv] session initialized: 993de60c-4ea1-4340-947f-68426b99e931
[srv] POST method=notifications/initialized mcp-session-id=993de60c-4ea1-4340-947f-68426b99e931
[srv] GET mcp-session-id=993de60c-4ea1-4340-947f-68426b99e931
[srv] POST method=tools/list mcp-session-id=993de60c-4ea1-4340-947f-68426b99e931
[srv] POST method=tools/call mcp-session-id=993de60c-4ea1-4340-947f-68426b99e931
[srv] POST method=notifications/cancelled mcp-session-id=993de60c-4ea1-4340-947f-68426b99e931
```

`mcp-session-id` was present on **every** post-initialize request, including the very first `tools/call`, and the call succeeded. Same result with and without custom `headers` in the config. This matches the SDK client code opencode uses (`StreamableHTTPClientTransport._commonHeaders()` captures `mcp-session-id` from the `initialize` response and attaches it to all subsequent requests).

Two deltas vs. the reported setup that may be where the divergence lives:

1. My harness is HTTP/1.1 (Node/express); the reported trace shows `HTTP/2 200`. If the failing server negotiates h2, it's worth checking whether the header is absent on the wire vs. present-but-rejected (h2 lowercases header names — a server matching `Mcp-Session-Id` case-sensitively would 400 exactly like this).
2. My harness uses the reference SDK server transport; the failing server is described as uvicorn-based, same family as #32809.

A wire capture (mitmproxy) of one failing `tools/call` distinguishing "header missing" from "header present but rejected" would pin this down — my data suggests the client does send it.

